+## Peer overlay (list changes straight between devices)
+
+A desktop that writes its list also sends the same ops to its online computers that
+advertise `registry-peer-v1`, as `PeerListOps` on a direct road whose link proved the
+sender. Never on the relay: that's the edge, which the room already is, and the relay's
+host refuses `PeerListOps` whoever calls (`crates/rpc/src/direct_only.rs`). The room stays
+the authority; this only shows a change sooner, and keeps the list moving while the edge is
+down.
+
+- The receiver takes a call only from a caller `crates/engine/src/direct_peer.rs` admits:
+  proven by its link (`Caller::Peer{device: Some(..)}`), another device, listed here, its key
+  not revoked in the account's keys kept here, not a Cloud machine. A Cloud machine takes
+  none (its list is cut to its reach). A device gets a burst of 32 calls, then 4 a second.
+  A call carrying more than 400 ops is refused before its ops are read.
+- It keeps an op only if every clock on it names the sender. It also refuses: an op the
+  room's `validateOp` would refuse (mirrored in Rust, a `delete` carrying `set` even as
+  `null` included); one clocked more than 5 minutes ahead of its own clock; a `devices` op
+  on any row but the sender's own, and any `devices` tombstone. It drops from an op the host
+  it names for a chat or Space (`deviceId`), unless the room hasn't got the row and the host
+  named is the sender: which device hosts a chat only ever comes from the room.
+- Taken ops live in memory alone (`crates/doc/src/registry/peer_overlay.rs`), at most 8 per
+  sender per row, 2,000 per sender and 8,000 in all, the oldest going first. A read of a row
+  replays its peer ops over the room's row, then the device's own pending ops on it, then
+  the peer ops again when it has own ones (a peer's update to a row only this device's
+  queue has made), with the same merge. They're never pushed, saved, or counted toward the
+  cursor.
+- An op stays only while it still changes the row as the room has it: a room row with the
+  same clock or a newer one drops it. Dropping an op that changes nothing at its place in
+  the replay doesn't change what's shown, and once the room has every op nothing's left.
+- One the room never confirms is dropped 10 minutes after it arrived while the room is
+  reachable and synced, and 6 hours after while it isn't.
+- An `update` this device writes to a row it knows only through a peer's ops stays in its
+  pending queue, unpushed, until the room has the row (the room drops an update to a row it
+  hasn't got, so a peer's create landing after it would lose the write). Later batches on
+  the same rows wait with it, so each row's writes reach the room in order. A frame that
+  brings the room's rows pushes what's no longer waiting. The wait doesn't end when the
+  peer's ops leave (expired, or dropped past a cap), and a peer that keeps writing the row
+  doesn't stretch it: it ends two minutes after the first push it held back (counted from
+  when the room was reachable), and the update goes; if no room ever gets the row, it drops
+  it, as before. The rows waited on are saved with the pending queue, so a restart during
+  the outage doesn't end the wait.
+- Every presence beat, a sender calls each such peer (an empty `PeerListOps` when there's
+  nothing new). The receiver counts any taken call as a presence beat from that device, by
+  its own clock, in the same freshest-heard cache the room's presence feeds, so the device
+  list's `lastSeenAt` and the dial gate see it with the edge down. A removed or revoked
+  device's calls are refused, so they count for nothing, and it's sent nothing. It never feeds the
+  room's live presence map, which alone drives the deaf-socket tripwire. A device that
+  stops beating goes offline after the usual window (45 s in the engine, 70 s in the app).
+- A sender sends a peer that may have missed some (no road a moment ago, a failed send or
+  beat, the first send, or its beats coming back after a silence) its whole pending queue
+  first, at the next beat or at once. The engine side is
+  `crates/engine/src/workspace_host/peer_list.rs` and `peer_presence.rs`.
